Transformer models utilize attention mechanisms within their architecture to process sequences. Each transformer layer employs self-attention, where the model computes attention scores for every pair of input tokens. These scores represent the relevance of one token to another, allowing the model to focus on the most important parts of the sequence simultaneously. This is achieved through scaled dot-product attention, which efficiently handles large sequences without exploding computational complexity. The weighted inputs from all positions are then combined with a learnable feed-forward network to produce output tokens. This process is parallel across all positions, enabling efficient and scalable processing of any input length. Transformers achieve this adaptability by allowing each token to attend to a subset of the entire input, making them highly effective for tasks like text generation and translation.
